---
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import NurseryStory from '../../components/NurseryStory.astro';
---

<CaseStudy
  slug="nursery-placement"
  facts={[
    ['Where', 'Trees for the Future, Kenya'],
    ['Tools', 'Python, GeoPandas, SRTM 30 m elevation'],
    ['Outputs', 'GeoJSON layers, farm-to-nursery CSV, interactive map, decision report'],
  ]}
>
  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">Problem</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Farmers collect their seedlings from a nursery on foot. The programme had to decide how many nurseries to run
        and where to put them.
      </p>
      <p>
        The usual shortcut is a ring drawn around each site. That ignores hills, and in this terrain a kilometre uphill
        and a kilometre on the flat are different walks.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">What I did</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I treated every mapped farm as both demand and a possible host. Farm polygons became centroids. For each pair
        of farms I worked out a walking time from the straight-line distance and the elevation difference between them,
        using SRTM 30 m elevation and Tobler's hiking function.
      </p>
      <p>
        A greedy set cover then picked the smallest group of host farms that reaches every farm inside the time limit.
        A pruning pass removed any site the others already covered.
      </p>
      <p>
        I ran it for networks of 264, 291, 352, 1,000 and 2,000 sites, so the programme could see what each step up in
        convenience would cost.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <NurseryStory />

  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">Result</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        On the real programme the lean network was 264 sites. It reached all 18,055 mapped farms within a 30-minute
        walk. The average farm-to-nursery distance was 1,317.3 m, and 61.49% of farms had a second nursery in range.
      </p>
      <p>
        The programme received GeoJSON layers, an interactive map, a CSV assigning every farm to a nursery, and a
        written report comparing the scenarios.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">Limits and next steps</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Paths are straight lines, so real walks are longer. Start points are farm centroids, not homesteads. Water,
        land tenure and whether a farmer is willing to host are not in the model. The site list is a planning anchor
        that still has to be checked on the ground.
      </p>
      <p>Next I would add recorded footpath traces and least-cost paths around rivers.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</CaseStudy>
